+10. When the same multi-step protocol appears in 3 or more workflow skills (or 3 or more call sites within one collapsed multi-stage skill — e.g. a router stage plus the stage references it delegates to), extract it into its own file. **Location:** a skill with only one or two patterns keeps them flat, named `pattern-<name>.md`, co-located with the skill (e.g. a hypothetical `some-skill/pattern-review-gate.md`). A skill with enough patterns to warrant it (three or more, or patterns alongside stage `references/`) uses a `patterns/` subfolder instead, dropping the prefix — the folder name already carries that meaning: `dev/patterns/review-gate.md`, `dev/patterns/task-tracking.md`. Pick one style per skill. Do **not** register pattern files in `CLAUDE.md` — they are loaded on-demand via explicit references, not at session start.
+11. Each workflow skill that uses a pattern must include an explicit path reference at the point of use: `"Apply the X pattern — read `.claude/skills/<skill>/patterns/X.md`."` (or `pattern-X.md` for a skill using the flat style). This is what triggers Claude to read it mid-execution. A name-only reference is not enough.
 12. Pattern files define the rule text once. Workflow skills own only the trigger condition (when to apply) and any local overrides (e.g. section name, tri-choice options). Never repeat the rule body in the workflow skill.
 13. When a pattern evolves, update the pattern file only. Workflow skills need to change only if their trigger condition or override changes — not because the rule text changed.
 
